# AMBE-3000F exchanges observed on the operator's bench, 2026-09-14.
#
# A DVMEGA DVstick 30 passed through ESXi to the production server
# (192.168.1.247), served by stock AMBEserver version 2015-07-11 at 460800
# baud on the by-id path, spoken to over UDP 2460 by cmd/ambe-probe from the
# Fedora development machine.
#
# One record per line: a name, then the packet as hex. Comments start with '#'.
#
# **These are recordings, not readings.** Everything in manual-examples.hex is
# the manufacturer's printed bytes; everything here is what this chip actually
# sent and received. Where the two agree, a claim is settled twice over.
#
# What this capture proved, none of it inferable:
#
#   - Speech in as type 0x02 produces a channel packet out as type 0x01.
#     Patch 0351 swapped those on a hypothesis and 0352 swapped them back on
#     the strength of a contents page. This is the wire.
#   - The length rule holds in the reply direction: channel-reply declares
#     0x000B field bytes and the whole packet is fifteen.
#   - PKT_RATET with index 33 took effect. CFG1 reads 0x00, so all six RATE
#     pins are low and the board boots at whatever Table 116 gives for a rate
#     control word of zero, which is not index 33. The returned frame carries
#     0x48 bits: 72 bits in 20 ms, which is 3600 bps. The rate was set by the
#     packet and the evidence is the frame size rather than the
#     acknowledgement.
#   - A control field is acknowledged by its own identifier followed by 0x00,
#     as Table 43 says.
#   - The configuration response parses. CFG0 0x05 is packet mode over UART
#     with companding disabled, so 16-bit linear samples. CFG2 0xec has
#     PARITY_ENABLE low, so parity is off -- and that pin has an internal
#     pullup, so floating it would read high and parity would be on. Reading
#     zero means the DVMEGA board ties it low deliberately, which is why stock
#     AMBEserver works on it at all.
#
# speech-request is 160 samples of a 1 kHz sine at amplitude 8000, generated
# by cmd/ambe-probe's own sine(). Its data repeats every eight samples, which
# is one cycle at 8 kHz.

reset-request 6100010033

reset-reply 6100010039

prodid-request 6100010030

prodid-reply 61000B0030414D4245333030304600

version-request 6100010031

version-reply 6100310031563132312E453130302E585858582E433131302E473531342E523031342E41303033303630382E433030323032303800

getcfg-request 6100010036

getcfg-reply 61000400360500EC

ratet-request 610002000921

ratet-reply 610002000900

speech-request 

channel-reply 61000B010148954BE6500310B00777

# Added 2026-09-14, later the same evening: the decode direction, which the
# earlier run had not exercised. `ambe-probe -decode 954be6500310b00777` sent
# the dongle's own channel frame back for decoding.
#
# **The framing worked and the audio did not.** A 326-byte output speech packet
# came back -- `61 01 42 02 00 a0` and 320 bytes, 160 samples, no CMODE field
# because none had been asked for -- so SpeechFromResponse is proved on
# hardware. But the peak sample is 3 where the frame had encoded a 1 kHz tone
# at amplitude 8000, and the data is dither around zero. That is comfort noise
# or nothing, and which of the candidates it is cannot be read off the samples.
#
# The reply itself is not stored. 320 bytes of near-silence is not something
# anything should assert against, and a truncated packet in a file of whole
# packets is a trap for whatever reads it next -- every record here is a
# complete datagram, and a length test over this file depends on that. Its
# header was `61 01 42 02 00 a0`: 0x0142 field bytes, which is the SPEECHD
# identifier, a count of 0xa0, and 320 bytes of samples.
#
# **Settled later the same evening, by asking the chip.** PKT_SPCHFMT was set
# so that output speech packets carry DCMODE_OUT, and the reply's tail became
# `02 00 02`: a CMODE field with VOICE_ACTIVE set, DATA_INVALID clear and
# TONE_FRAME clear. The chip is reporting a valid voice frame faithfully
# decoded -- not comfort noise, not a frame repeat, not FEC errors, not a tone.
#
# So nothing was broken. An AMBE encoder carries state, the frame above was the
# first out of a freshly reset one, and `954be6500310b00777` is a valid
# encoding of approximately silence. Decoding it ten times running gave peaks
# of 3, 1 and then zero for the remaining eight, which is the decoder settling
# on the silence it was given.
#
# **The method was wrong rather than the code.** A vocoder is tested with a
# stream, which is also what QSP sends: fifty frames a second for the length of
# a transmission. `ambe-probe -roundtrip N` does that.

decode-request 61000C01400148954BE6500310B00777

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The audio round trip, 2026-09-14, fifty consecutive frames.
#
# **It works.** `ambe-probe -roundtrip 50` encoded fifty 20 ms frames of a
# 1 kHz tone at amplitude 8000 as a continuous stream, then decoded them back
# in the order they were produced. Output peaks: 683, 7293, 8080, and then
# between 7944 and 8796 for the remaining forty-seven. **Warm-up is two
# frames**, which is what the near-silent single-frame result was all along.
# The slight overshoot above 8000 is a lossy resynthesis reconstructing a
# spectral model, and it is nowhere near the 32767 that would clip.
#
# **And the run was at the wrong rate, because the probe skipped its own
# precondition.** The frames were six bytes -- 48 bits, 2400 bps, Table 115
# index 0, which is this board's boot rate with every RATE pin low. The
# -roundtrip code path returned before the block that sent PKT_RATET and
# PKT_SPCHFMT, which is why every row read "no flags requested" too. The same
# program had printed, four lines earlier, that setting the rate is a
# precondition for audio and not a refinement.
#
# The rate acknowledgement could not have caught it: `09 00` says a field
# arrived. **The frame width is the only evidence of the rate in effect**, so
# Table 115 is now in the tree as TotalRates and the round trip refuses a frame
# whose width disagrees with the index it asked for.
#
# Frames are not stored here. Fifty six-byte frames at a rate nobody wants are
# not a fixture; the two frames worth keeping are channel-reply above, at 72
# bits, and whatever the first correct 72-bit run produces.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same run at the DMR rate, 2026-09-14, and what it actually proved.
#
# Fifty frames at rate index 33: 72 bits each, output peaks 8548 to 8611
# against an input of 8000 from frame 2 onward. **The round trip works at the
# rate QSP wants**, and frame 1 came back as `954be6500310b00777` -- bit for
# bit the frame captured hours earlier from the same input. The chip is
# deterministic from a reset, which makes every future capture comparable.
#
# **But frames 2 to 50 were byte-identical**, `ece88ce928ac00c442`, and the
# decoder reported `decoded as a tone frame` on every one. TD_ENABLE is
# initialised to 1 at reset whatever the configuration pins say (Table 13), so
# the encoder recognised the steady 1 kHz sine as a tone and emitted a tone
# descriptor. Speech coded at 3600 bps changes every 20 ms; a descriptor does
# not.
#
# So the tone path is proved and **the voice path is not**. A steady sine is
# the wrong signal for a speech coder, which the bench showed rather than
# anybody arguing. Two ways to separate them, one variable each:
# `-signal sweep` changes the input and no setting, and `-tone-detect=false`
# changes one bit -- 0x1000 to 0x0000 on this board, since every pin-derived
# bit is already low -- and no input.
#
# Neither run has been taken yet. The frames are not stored: fifty copies of
# one tone descriptor is one fact, and it is written above.
